  "name": "FRACTIONAL EXCRETION OF SODIUM",
  "code": "1103",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p>The <strong data-start=\"140\" data-end=\"182\">Fractional Excretion of Sodium (FeNa%)</strong> test measures the percentage of filtered sodium that is excreted in the urine. It is a valuable tool in distinguishing <strong data-start=\"302\" data-end=\"314\">prerenal</strong> causes of acute kidney injury (AKI) from <strong data-start=\"356\" data-end=\"375\">intrinsic renal</strong> causes (especially acute tubular necrosis - ATN).</p>\n<p>Elevated FeNa% &ndash; Clinical Implications :</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>FeNa% Value</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Interpretation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>&gt;2%</strong></td>\n<td>Suggests <strong>acute tubular necrosis (ATN)</strong> or intrinsic renal failure &ndash; the kidneys are unable to reabsorb sodium efficiently due to tubular damage.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>1&ndash;2%</strong></td>\n<td>Indeterminate zone &ndash; may occur in evolving ATN, early recovery, or mixed picture.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>&gt;2% in chronic kidney disease (CKD)</strong></td>\n<td>Not always diagnostic; CKD patients may have altered sodium handling.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
